{"annotations":{"destructiveHint":false,"idempotentHint":true,"openWorldHint":false,"readOnlyHint":true},"description":"Reads one turn of a session: the user prompt and assistant response (secrets redacted, truncated to maxTextChars), its token usage and AI Credits, any triggered event (compaction, model switch, cache expiry, ...) and its tool calls with outcomes. The text and tool calls are unknown, with a reason, for a VS Code Agent Host turn whose Copilot CLI event log is missing, unreadable or has no match. Use it to explain why a turn found by get_session_cost or list_tool_calls was expensive or failed. `cost` gives the turn's billed, reported and priced figures (the priced one names its price version) and `preferredCost` the one totals use. In a VS Code Copilot Chat or Claude Code session the usage includes the subagent runs the turn started: `usage.subagents` lists them and `usage.ownUsage` is the main agent's own. A run that cannot be read or placed makes the figures unknown, with a reason, never 0. Claude Code runs started off the active branch (after an edit or rewind) are not counted. Each tool call says whether the main agent or a subagent run made it (`origin`). Cost figures carry their basis: billed (what GitHub Copilot billed, AI Credits), reported (what the agent logged itself, USD) and priced (tokens \u00d7 the price version in force on the turn's channel at the turn's time; see get_price_book, and priceVersionIds). The preferred figure is billed, else reported, else priced. A priced figure with apiEquivalent: true is what the same use costs on the API, though a flat-fee plan may have covered it. A priced figure with userPriceVersionIds used price versions the user added themselves (not bundled prices); say so when you quote it. An unknown figure is {known:false, reason}, never 0; totals give knownTurns of turns per basis. Currencies stay apart; `usd` converts only at 1 AI Credit = $0.01.","inputSchema":{"$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020